When the page has no handler for an action, Ladybird applies it
to the active document’s controlled media, and never rewinds on
stop. Chromium does the same for play, pause, and seeking, in
MediaSessionImpl::Resume(), Suspend(), and Seek(), and so does Gecko,
in ContentPlaybackController. Ladybird’s stop pauses and then ends
the session, as Chromium’s MediaSessionImpl::Stop() does, and as Gecko
does by unregistering the stopped media (ContentMediaController::
PauseOrStopMedia()); WebKit only pauses. WebKit sends the
command to its selected media element, in HTMLMediaElement::
didReceiveRemoteControlCommand().

Ladybird’s default play resumes only the media its default pause paused,
like Gecko’s ContentMediaController. Without an offset in the action,
Ladybird seeks by 15 seconds, WebKit’s default interval for relative
skips (RemoteCommandListenerCocoa); Chromium and Gecko apply only the
interval the command carries.

Ladybird grants user activation before it runs the page’s handler,
or the default. Blink grants it for every action but pause and
stop (MediaSession::DidReceiveAction()), and WebKit runs both
with a user-gesture indicator, while Gecko’s MediaSession::
DispatchNotifyHandler() grants none.

These comparisons come from reading each engine’s source, at Chromium
8658d682eac5, WebKit 047a16c96ccd, and Gecko 06fa2674be3d: What an
engine hands the OS media controls isn’t observable from page script, so
it couldn’t be compared by running them.
